= Interns
Short research internships in the group

We host a small number of interns every year. Internships are usually short projects (8--16
weeks) that can be done alongside coursework, and they are the most common way to join the
group later as an honors or Master student.

== Current interns

- *Example Intern One* \M since 2026.\n
  Project: simulation of RIS-assisted links.\n
  Email: [intern.one@example.edu]
- *Example Intern Two* \M since 2026.\n
  Project: survey and reproduction of baseline scheduling algorithms.\n
  Email: [intern.two@example.edu]

== Former interns

- *Example Intern Three* (2025). Reproduced a baseline power-control scheme; continued as an
  honors student.
- *Example Intern Four* (2024). Implemented a small test-bed for channel measurements.

== What an internship looks like

- *Duration*: 8--16 weeks, 8--12 hours per week.
- *Starting point*: a well-defined sub-problem with a concrete deliverable
  (a simulation, a reproducible experiment, or a short technical note).
- *What you get*: weekly supervision, access to our simulation code, and a reference letter
  if the work goes well.
- *How to apply*: see the [prospective.html Prospective Students] page.

